<wpf:ResourceDictionary xml:space="preserve" xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml" xmlns:s="clr-namespace:System;assembly=mscorlib" xmlns:ss="urn:shemas-jetbrains-com:settings-storage-xaml" xmlns:wpf="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation">
	<!--
		Solution-shared ReSharper/CLT settings layer. This file sits next to PetBox.slnx and is
		picked up automatically by name (PetBox.slnx.DotSettings) — both `jb inspectcode` /
		`jb cleanupcode` and Rider mount it as a layer, no project reference or config flag needed.

		TWO THINGS THAT COST US A FULL DEBUGGING SESSION, recorded here so nobody re-derives them:

		1. A cleanup PROFILE IS NOT A TREE OF BOOLEAN KEYS. It looks like it should be
		   `/Default/CodeStyle/CodeCleanup/Profiles/=PetBoxSafe/CSOptimizeUsings/OptimizeUsings` with
		   a bool value next to it — that is how most other ReSharper settings are shaped, and it is
		   the wrong shape here. A profile is ONE key, `.../Profiles/=<name>/@EntryIndexedValue`, and
		   its value is an s:String holding an entire escaped XML document (`<Profile>...</Profile>`,
		   HTML-entity-escaped: `<` becomes `&lt;` etc.). Anything written as a nested-key tree is
		   silently ignored — no error, no warning, the profile just behaves as if the setting were
		   never made. The `name="..."` inside the embedded XML and the `=<name>` in the outer key
		   must match byte-for-byte (spaces in a profile name would encode as `_0020` in the key, but
		   "PetBoxSafe" has none).

		2. INSPECTCODE/CLEANUPCODE CACHE SOLUTIONS AND DO NOT INVALIDATE ON SETTINGS CHANGES.
		   CLT keeps a persistent per-solution cache under
		   `%LOCALAPPDATA%\JetBrains\Transient\InspectCode\v262\SolutionCaches\_PetBox.*` (cleanupcode
		   uses the sibling `Transient\CleanupCode` folder). Editing THIS file, or .editorconfig, does
		   NOT bust that cache: a run against a stale cache returns the same findings as before the
		   edit, which looks exactly like "my setting had no effect" even when it did. The only fixes
		   are a `caches-home` option pointed at a folder scoped to the current settings, or deleting
		   the cache folder outright. scripts/inspect-gate.cs derives its `caches-home` value from a
		   hash of this file plus .editorconfig for exactly this reason — see the comment there.

		Both of the above were confirmed with a negative control (profile absent/wrong shape found
		nothing; identical settings behind a freshly scoped cache folder changed the result). Treat
		them as facts, not folklore.

		(NOTE for anyone editing THIS comment block: a literal double hyphen anywhere inside an XML
		comment is illegal and breaks the parse of the WHOLE file silently for these tools — the
		settings loader does not surface an error, it just behaves as if none of the keys below were
		ever set. Spell CLI flag names out in prose without their two leading hyphens together, the
		way this comment block does throughout.)
	-->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeStyle/CodeCleanup/Profiles/=PetBoxSafe/@EntryIndexedValue">&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-16"?&gt;&lt;Profile name="PetBoxSafe"&gt;&lt;CSOptimizeUsings&gt;&lt;OptimizeUsings&gt;True&lt;/OptimizeUsings&gt;&lt;EmbraceInRegion&gt;False&lt;/EmbraceInRegion&gt;&lt;RegionName&gt;&lt;/RegionName&gt;&lt;/CSOptimizeUsings&gt;&lt;/Profile&gt;</s:String>
	<!--
		Use it explicitly, never as the implicit default profile (Rider/CLT default to "Full
		Cleanup", which reformats and reorders far more than we want in a gate):

		  jb cleanupcode PetBox.slnx, with the profile option set to PetBoxSafe (see
		  scripts/inspect-gate.cs usage header, or this file's own comments above, for why this
		  comment spells CLI flags out in prose instead of typing their leading hyphens directly)

		Right now this profile does exactly one thing — remove unused usings — on purpose. It is
		the smallest cleanup that is safe to run unattended across the whole solution; anything
		riskier (reformatting, member reordering, "make readonly", etc.) belongs in a reviewed,
		file-scoped pass, not in a profile anyone can invoke solution-wide.
	-->

	<!--
		CS8602 (possible null dereference of `last` in MemoryTools.cs, around the nextCursor
		construction) — SEVERITY OVERRIDE, not a suppression of a single finding.
		`InspectionSeverities/=<rule>` is solution-wide: it changes how ReSharper (Rider included)
		and inspectcode both treat this rule everywhere, which is exactly what we want here, because
		this is not a one-off false positive but a structural disagreement between two analyzers.

		CS-prefixed codes are COMPILER diagnostics, not ReSharper inspections. Directory.Build.props
		sets TreatWarningsAsErrors=true, and inspectcode honors that by reporting CS-codes at ERROR
		severity too — so this same finding is also something `dotnet build` would report, and would
		report first, and more precisely, if it were real.

		Concretely, at MemoryTools.cs (`nextCursor` construction): `last` is read only inside
		`kept.Count > 0 ? ordered[kept.Count - 1] : default`, i.e. exactly the branch where `last`
		was just assigned from `ordered[kept.Count - 1]` rather than left at `default`. Roslyn's
		nullable-flow analysis threads that guard through to the read on the next line; ReSharper's
		solution-wide analysis does not carry the same guard across the two expressions and reports
		a possible-null-dereference that the compiler itself considers proven safe. If this were a
		real bug, `dotnet build` — which actually gates merges — would already be red on it.

		Setting this to DO_NOT_SHOW mutes CS8602 for the ENTIRE solution, not just this call site.
		That is a deliberate, solution-wide trade — accepting that inspectcode will never again be
		the tool that catches a genuine CS8602 — made because the compiler already owns that
		diagnostic end to end. It is not an oversight and not a narrow suppression; if a future
		finding under this rule turns out to be real, `dotnet build` is expected to have already
		failed on it. Do not read this as "we didn't get around to fixing it."

		For a narrower case — accepting N specific existing occurrences of a rule while still
		failing on NEW ones — use the `suppressions` baseline array in scripts/inspect-gate.cs
		instead of widening a rule's severity here.
	-->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=CSharpWarnings_003A_003ACS8602/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>
</wpf:ResourceDictionary>
